          %p#HomotopicalPerspectivesOnTDAJune2022
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} Homotopical
              perspectives on
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+data+analysis"} Topological data analysis
          %p
            Organizers:
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sadok+Kallel"} Sadok Kallel
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hisham+Sati"} Hisham Sati
          %p
            %br/
          %div{:style => "margin: -60px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/HomotopicalPerspectivesOnTDAatNYUAD-May2022.jpg", :width => "510px"}/
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            Schedule for
            = succeed ":" do
              %strong 02 June 2022
          %p
            %br/
          %ul
            %li
              %p#ZhouAtHomotopicalPerspectivesOnTDAJune2022
                15:00 - 16:00
                %a{:href => "https://www.timeanddate.com/time/zones/gst"} GST/UTC+4
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ling+Zhou"} Ling Zhou
                (The Ohio State University, USA)
              %p
                %strong Persistent homotopy groups of metric spaces
              %blockquote
                %p
                  By capturing both geometric and topological features of datasets,
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/persistent+homology"} persistent homology
                  has shown its promise in applications. Motivated by the fact that
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopy
                  in general contains more information than
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homology"} homology
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/persistent+homotopy"} persistent homotopy
                  groups of compact
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+spaces"} metric spaces
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+group"} fundamental group
                  has an underlying tree-like structure (i.e. a dendrogram) and an associated ultrametric. We then exhibit pairs of filtrations that are confounded by
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/persistent+homology"} persistent homology
                  but are distinguished by their
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/persistent+homotopy"} persistent homotopy
                  groups. We finally describe the notion of persistent
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+homotopy+theory"} rational
                  homotopy groups, which is easier to handle but still contains extra information compared to persistent homology.
              %p
                %br/
            %li
              %p
                16:00 - 17:00
                %a{:href => "https://www.timeanddate.com/time/zones/gst"} GST/UTC+4
              %p#ChacholskiTalkAtHomotopicalPerspectivesOnTDA2022
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wojciech+Chacholski"} Wojciech Chacholski
                (KTH, Sweden)
              %p
                %strong Realisations of Posets
              %blockquote
                %p
                  My presentation is based on an article with the same title coauthored with A. Jin and F. Tombari (
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.12209"}> arXiv:2112.12209
                  ).
                %p
                  Encoding information in form of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functors"} functors
                  indexed by the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} poset
                  of
                  = succeed "-tuples" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi r
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/persistence+modules"} persistence modules
                %p a) metric properties of the poset are essential to study distances on persistence modules
                %p
                  b) the poset of
                  = succeed "-tuples" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi r
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
                %p c) the mentioned discretizations and approximations have well studied algebraic and homological properties as they can be identified with multi graded modules over polynomial rings.
                %p
                  In my talk I will describe a construction called realisation, that transforms arbitrary posets into posets which satisfy all three requirements above and hence are particularly suitable for persistence methods.Intuitively the realisation associates a continuous structure to a locally discrete poset by filling in empty spaces. For example the realisation of the poset of natural numbers is the poset of non-negative reals. I will focus on illustrating how
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+algebra"} homological techniques
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Koszul+complexes"} Koszul complexes
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/persistence+modules"} persistence modules
                  indexed by realisations.
              %p
                %br/
            %li
              %p#GinotAtHomotopicalPerspectivesOnTDAJune2022
                17:30 - 18:30
                %a{:href => "https://www.timeanddate.com/time/zones/gst"} GST/UTC+4
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gr%C3%A9gory+Ginot"} Grégory Ginot
                (Université Paris 13, France)
              %p
                %strong Homotopical and sheaf theoretic point of view on multi-parameter persistence.
              %blockquote
                %p
                  In this talk we will highlight the study of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/level+set"} level set
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/persistent+homology"} persistence
                  through the prism of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf+theory"} sheaf theory
                  and a special type of 2-parameter persistence: Mayer-Vietoris systems and a pseudo-symetry between those. This is based on
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a{:href => "persistent+homology#BerkoukGinotOudot19"} joint work with Berkouk and Oudot
              %p
                %br/
            %li
              %p#JardineAtHomotopicalPerspectivesOnTDAJune2022
                18:30 - 19:30
                %a{:href => "https://www.timeanddate.com/time/zones/gst"} GST/UTC+4
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rick+Jardine"} Rick Jardine
                (University of Western Ontario, Canada)
              %p
                %strong Thoughts on big data sets
              %blockquote
                %p
                  This talk describes work in progress. The idea is to develop methods for analyzing a very large data sets
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                        %mo ⊂
                        %msup
                          %mi ℝ
                          %mi N
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \subset \mathbb{R}^{N}
                  in high dimensional spaces. There are well-known pitfalls to avoid, including the inability to computationally analyze
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TDA"} TDA
                  constructions for
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                  on account of its size, the “curse of high dimensionality”, and the failure of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excision"} excision
                  for standard
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TDA"} TDA
                  constructions. We discuss the curse of high dimensionality and define a hypercube metric on
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi ℝ
                          %mi N
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^{N}
                  that may lessen its effects. The excision problem for the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vietoris-Rips+complex"} Vietoris-Rips construction
                  can be addressed by expanding the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TDA"} TDA
                  discussion to
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/filtered+object"} filtered
                  subobjects
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi K
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
                  of Vietoris-Rips constructions. Unions of such subobjects satisfy excision in path components (clusters) and
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homology+groups"} homology groups
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi K
                          %mi x
                        %mo ⊂
                        %mi V
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_{x} \subset V(X)
                  with
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                          %mo ∈
                          %msub
                            %mi K
                            %mi x
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in K_{x}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                  near
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                  = succeed "-nearest" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi k
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi X
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi K
                            %mi x
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_{x}
