          %p
            %br/
          %ul
            %li#KhovanovMar2023
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mikhail+Khovanov"} Mikhail Khovanov
                (Columbia University):
              %p
                %strong Universal construction, foams and link homology
              %p
                cf.:
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.09662"} arXiv:1808.09662
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2011.11077"} arXiv:2011.11077
              %p
                video 1:
                %a{:href => "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Urh1NNcbCcc"}> YT
                , 2:
                %a{:href => "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SPBcAAmGT88"} YT
              %blockquote
                %p
                  In this series of three talks we will explain the foam approach to
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/link+homology"} link homology
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorification"} categorify
                  the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jones+polynomial"} Jones polynomial
                  and other
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Reshetikhin-Turaev+construction"} Reshetikhin-Turaev
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/link+invariants"} link invariants
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/HOMFLY-PT+polynomial"} HOMFLY-PT polynomial
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polyhedron"} polyhedral
                  2D
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cell+complex"} complexes
                  embedded in
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+space"} 3-space
                  allow to construct state spaces for
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/planar+graphs"} planar graphs
                  which are then used to define link homology groups. The most explicit and efficient way to define graph state spaces is via evaluation of the closed foams (Robert-Wagner formula).
                %p
                  A) This formula will be first explained in the less technical unoriented
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi SL
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 3
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SL(3)
                  case. Resulting graph state spaces are then related to the Four-Color Theorem and Kronheimer-Mrowka homology for 3-
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbifolds"}> orbifolds
                  \.
                %p
                  B) A step in that construction requires building a topological theory (a lax TQFT) from an evaluation of closed objects, such as closed
                  = succeed "-manifolds." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+languages"} regular languages
                  and
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automata"} automata
                %p
                  C) Robert-Wagner
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi GL
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi N
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} GL(N)
                  foam evaluation and its application to constructing link homology theories will be explained as well.
          %p
            %br/
          %ul
            %li#KitchlooMar2023
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nitu+Kitchloo"} Nitu Kitchloo
                (John Hopkins University):
              %p
                %strong Symmetry breaking and homotopy types for link homologies
              %p
                cf.:
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.07443"} arXiv:1910.07443
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.07444"} arXiv:1910.07444
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.07516"} arXiv:1910.07516
              %p
                video:
                %a{:href => "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=StRyIjpER9I"} YT
              %blockquote
                %p
                  I will describe how the spaces that record
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetry+breaking"} symmetry breaking
                  data in a
                  = succeed "-" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi U
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi n
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(n)
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
                  (for arbitrary
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+types"} homotopy types
                  that are
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/link+invariant"} invariants for links
                  in
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi ℝ
                            %mn 3
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^3
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Khovanov-Rozansky+link+homology"} Khovanov-Rozansky link homology
                  and
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝔰𝔩
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{sl}(n)
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/link+homology"} link homology
                  by evaluating this homotopy type under suitable
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Whitehead-generalized+cohomology"} cohomology theories
          %p
