                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Berwick-Evans"} Daniel Berwick-Evans
                (University of Illinois Urbana-Champaign)
              %p
                %strong How do field theories detect the torsion in topological modular forms?
              %p
                cf.:
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.09138"} arXiv:2303.09138
              %p
                video:
                %a{:href => "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fw7yxFsDmjs"} YT
              %blockquote
                %p
                  Since the mid 1980s, there have been hints of a deep connection between
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+CFT"} 2-dimensional
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} field theories
                  and
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+cohomology"} elliptic cohomology
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/What+is+an+elliptic+object%3F"} Stolz and Teichner's conjectured geometric model
                  for the universal
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+cohomology"} elliptic cohomology
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Whitehead-generalized+cohomology"} theory
                  of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+modular+forms"} topological modular forms
                  (
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TMF"}> TMF
                  ) in which
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocycles"} cocycles
                  are
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%282%2C1%29-dimensional+Euclidean+field+theory"} 2-dimensional supersymmetric field theories
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torsion+subgroup"} torsion
                  in
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TMF"} TMF
                  has always been mysterious. In this talk, I will describe deformation invariants of 2-dimensional field theories that realize certain torsion classes in TMF. This leads to a description of the generator of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tmf#HomotopyGroups"}
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi π
                            %mn 3
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi TMF
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo =
                          %mi ℤ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mn 24
