                (RWTH Aachen University and Forschungszentrum Jülich, Germany):
              %p
                %strong Fault-Tolerant Topological Quantum Computing: From Concepts to Experiments
              %blockquote
                %p
                  To date, the construction of scalable fault-tolerant
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+computers"} quantum computers
                  remains a fundamental scientific and technological challenge, due to the influence of unavoidable
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noise"} noise
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+error+correction+codes"} quantum error correction codes
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+error+correction"} quantum error correction
                  on various physical quantum computing platforms. This includes the first realisation of repeated high-performance quantum error-correction cycles on a topological surface code with
                  %a{:href => "superconductivity#SuperconductingQBitsReferences"} superconducting qubits
                  [1], and the first demonstration of a universal and fault-tolerant logical gate set with
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trapped+ions"} trapped ions
                  [2]. Furthermore, I will present new fundamental connections between topological
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+error+correction"} quantum error correction
                  and classical statistical mechanics models, in the context of the correction of qubit loss [3,4] and the determination of fundamental error thresholds for circuit noise [5].
                %p References
