          %p#RunningHoTT24Talks
            %strong Talks:
          %ul
            %li#AltenkirchApr2024
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thorsten+Altenkirch"} Thorsten Altenkirch
                (Univ. Nottingham):
              %p
                %strong Univalence Without an Interval
              %p
                video:
                %a{:href => "https://cdnapisec.kaltura.com/html5/html5lib/v2.73.2/mwEmbedFrame.php/p/1674401/uiconf_id/23435151/entry_id/1_21y98pg5?wid=_1674401&iframeembed=true&playerId=kaltura_player&entry_id=1_21y98pg5"} kt
              %blockquote
                %p
                  We are developing a new formulation of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence"} univalent
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
                  (known as
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/HoTT"} HoTT
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval+type"} interval type
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
                  with internal parametricity which can be modelled using BCH cubes - see our POPL paper [1]. As a next step we identify a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibrant+type"} fibrant
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+universe"} universe
                  using a higher
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coinductive+type"} coinductive type
                %p
                  This is joint work with Yorgo Chamoun,
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ambrus+Kaposi"} Ambrus Kaposi
                  and
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Shulman"} Mike Shulman
                %p
                  [1] Altenkirch, T., Chamoun, Y., Kaposi, A., &amp; Shulman, M. (2024).
                  %em
                    %a{:href => "higher+observational+type+theory#AltenkirchChamounKapoilsiShulman24"} Internal parametricity, without an interval
                  Proceedings of the ACM on Programming Languages (POPL)
                  %strong 8
                  (2024) 2340-2369 &amp;lbrack;
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.06448"}> arXiv:2307.06448
                  ,
                  = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                    %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1145/3632920"} doi:10.1145/3632920
            %li#AngiuliApr2024
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Carlo+Angiuli"} Carlo Angiuli
                (Indiana Univ.)
              %p
                %strong Normalization: Running Open Terms
              %p
                video:
                %a{:href => "https://cdnapisec.kaltura.com/html5/html5lib/v2.73.2/mwEmbedFrame.php/p/1674401/uiconf_id/23435151/entry_id/1_1wowhsfj?wid=_1674401&iframeembed=true&playerId=kaltura_player&entry_id=1_1wowhsfj"} kt
              %blockquote
                %p
                  This talk will survey the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normal+form"} normalization
                  problem for
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Shulman"} Shulman
                  and
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andr%C3%A1s+Kov%C3%A1cs"} Kovács
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simply-typed+lambda+calculus"} simply-typed lambda calculus
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/evaluation"} evaluation
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+types"} dependent types
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computation"} computation
                  and normalization, and a forthcoming expository resource.
            %li#BuchholtzApr2024
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ulrik+Buchholtz"} Ulrik Buchholtz
                (Univ. Nottingham)
              %p
                %strong Primitive recursive (homotopy) type theory
              %p
                video:
                %a{:href => "https://cdnapisec.kaltura.com/html5/html5lib/v2.73.2/mwEmbedFrame.php/p/1674401/uiconf_id/23435151/entry_id/1_neel5sdr?wid=_1674401"} kt
              %blockquote
                %p
                  I’ll present a subsystem of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin-L%C3%B6f+type+theory"} Martin-Löf type theory
                  where all
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+type"} functions
                  from
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers+type"} Nat
                  to
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers+type"} Nat
                  are
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/primitive+recursive+function"} primitive recursive
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Artin+gluing"} gluing
                  construction for a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf+topos"} sheaf topos
                  model constructed from a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
                  of primitive recursive functions. Then I’ll use this as a starting point for some speculations about
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+type+theory"} geometric type theory
                  and its computational aspects. This is based on joint work with Johannes Schipp von Branitz.
            %li#FinsterApr2024
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eric+Finster"} Eric Finster
                (Univ. Birmingham):
              %p
                %strong A Tour of Parameterized Spectra
              %p
                video:
                %a{:href => "https://cdnapisec.kaltura.com/p/1674401/sp/167440100/embedIframeJs/uiconf_id/23435151/partner_id/1674401?iframeembed=true&playerId=kaltura_player&entry_id=1_jf6fy0f2"} kt
              %blockquote
                %p
                  This talk will be a survey of some of the defining characteristics of the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+%28infinity%2C1%29-topos"} infinity-topos of
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/parameterized+spectra"} parameterized spectra
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
                  in the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+language"} internal language
                  of this topos.
            %li#GratzerApr2024
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Gratzer"} Daniel Gratzer
                (Aarhus Univ.):
              %p
                %strong Towards a category of spaces in simplicial type theory
              %p
                video:
                %a{:href => "https://cdnapisec.kaltura.com/p/1674401/sp/167440100/embedIframeJs/uiconf_id/23435151/partner_id/1674401?iframeembed=true&playerId=kaltura_player&entry_id=1_pe2rgyd4\n"} kt
              %blockquote
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+type+theory"} Simplicial type theory
                  as
                  %a{:href => "simplicial+type+theory#RiehlShulman17"} introduced by Riehl and Shulman
                  and developed by
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ulrik+Buchholtz"} Buchholtz
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type-theoretic
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+mathematics"} synthetic
                  study of
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-categories"} ∞-categories
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-groupoid"} groupoids
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+left+fibration"} universal left fibration
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+universe"} universe
                  of ∞-groupoids within an extension of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+homotopy+type+theory"} multimodal HoTT/homotopy MTT
                  tooled for synthetic cubical spaces and argue that it lies within the subcategory of (synthetic) simplicial spaces and satisfies the expected properties (
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+Segal+space"}> Segalness
                  ,
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rezk+completion"} Rezkness
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+univalence+axiom"} directed univalence
            %li{:id => "KovácsApr2024"}
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andr%C3%A1s+Kov%C3%A1cs"} András Kovács
                (Univ. Gothenburg):
              %p
                %strong Efficient Evaluation for Cubical Type Theories
              %p
                video:
                %a{:href => "https://cdnapisec.kaltura.com/html5/html5lib/v2.73.2/mwEmbedFrame.php/p/1674401/uiconf_id/23435151/entry_id/1_bdom5cdn?wid=_1674401&iframeembed=true&playerId=kaltura_player&entry_id=1_bdom5cdn"} kt
              %p
                slides:
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Kovacs-Apr2024.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
              %blockquote
                %p
                  There are numerous interesting computations in
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubical+type+theory"} cubical type theory
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+homotopy+theory"} synthetic homotopy theory
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/definitions"} definitions
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubical+type+theory"} CTT
                  with major performance improvements. Broadly speaking, the benefits stem from a) systematically omitting unnecessary computation b) exploiting the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonicity"} canonicity
                  property of the CTT when computation depends on
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval"} interval
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variables"} variables
                  but not
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+variables"} free variables
                  with
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibrant+types"} fibrant types
            %li#LamiauxApr2024
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thomas+Lamiaux"} Thomas Lamiaux
                (ENS Paris):
              %p
                %strong Computing Cohomology Rings in Cubical Agda
              %p
                video:
                %a{:href => "https://cdnapisec.kaltura.com/p/1674401/sp/167440100/embedIframeJs/uiconf_id/23435151/partner_id/1674401?iframeembed=true&playerId=kaltura_player&entry_id=1_2bxmtp75"} kt
              %blockquote
                %p
                  In this talk, we will discuss the formalization of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+rings"} cohomology rings
                  in
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a{:href => "Agda#CubicalAgda"} Cubical Agda
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/data+type"} data type
                  representation influence formalisation, in our case for representing
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polynomials"} polynomials
                  and
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+rings"} cohomology rings
                  2. How suitable representations can be used to compute cohomology rings 3. Where computation would be helpful, how it is limited in practice, and how we can still recover some computation to help us with our
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proof"} proof
            %li#LeeApr2024
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dongho+Lee"} Dongho Lee
                (Dalhousie Univ.)
              %p
                %strong A Concrete Categorical Semantics for Proto-Quipper Language and Dynamic Lifting
              %p
                slides:
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Lee-Apr2024.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
              %p
                video:
                %a{:href => "https://cdnapisec.kaltura.com/p/1674401/sp/167440100/embedIframeJs/uiconf_id/23435151/partner_id/1674401?iframeembed=true&playerId=kaltura_player&entry_id=1_ylwdptd4"} kt
              %blockquote
                %p
                  In this talk, we discuss a concrete
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
                  of
                  = succeed "-L," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Proto-Quipper"} Proto-Quipper
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+circuit"} quantum circuit
                  description language with
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dynamic+lifting"} dynamic lifting
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+lambda-calculus"} linear lambda-calculus
                  with
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+channel"} quantum channel
                  constants (which represent tree structure of
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gates"} gates
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+circuit"} circuit
                  operations (which are box and unbox) where
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terms"} terms
                  can have tree shapes. The
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+system"} type system
                  roughly comes from the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multiplicative+intuitionistic+linear+logic"} multiplicative intuitionistic linear logic
                  where the term represents the proof. The
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
                  of the language is built upon the linear and non-linear model for linear logic
                  %a{:href => "linear+type+theory#TheCanonicalCoModality"} by Benton
                  and coproduct completion as the categorical semantics of
                  = succeed "-M" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Proto-Quipper"} Proto-Quipper
                  %a{:href => "Quipper#RiosSelinger18"} by Rios and Selinger
                  while we give a concrete categorical model for diagrams based on graphical calculus. For the computational effect of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dynamic+lifting"} dynamic lifting
                  we introduce branching monad and give an interpretation of terms in the
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kleisli+category"} Kleisli category
            %li{:id => "LjungströmApr2024"}
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Axel+Ljungstr%C3%B6m"}> Axel Ljungström
                \:
              %p
                %strong More cellular (co)homology in HoTT
              %p
                video:
                %a{:href => "https://cdnapisec.kaltura.com/html5/html5lib/v2.73.2/mwEmbedFrame.php/p/1674401/uiconf_id/23435151/entry_id/1_r5x4tca9?wid=_1674401&iframeembed=true&playerId=kaltura_player&entry_id=1_r5x4tca9"} kt
              %blockquote
                %p
                  In this talk, I’ll present some ongoing work with
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Anders+M%C3%B6rtberg"} Anders Mörtberg
                  and
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lo%C3%AFc+Pujet"} Loïc Pujet
                  on the development and computer formalisation of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cellular+homology"} cellular homology
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cellular+cohomology"} and cohomology
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/HoTT"} HoTT
                  by
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ulrik+Buchholtz"} Buchholtz
                  and
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Favonia"} Favonia
                  who constructed cellular cohomology groups and showed that these define a
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+theory"} cohomology theory
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-MacLane+spaces"} Eilenberg-MacLane spaces
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cellular+approximations"} cellular approximations
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+homology"} homology
                  and
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+cohomology"} cohomology
