                %a{:href => "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=40_UDRGRP3Q"} YT
              %blockquote
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} Quantum
                  technology is a rapidly advancing field that is poised to revolutionize numerous industries, including
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+computing"} computing
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+sensing"} sensing
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cryptography"} cryptography
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+technology"} quantum technology
                  relies on the principles of
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+technology"} quantum technology
                  can perform tasks that are impossible or prohibitively difficult for
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+physics"} classical
                  devices. One of the most promising applications of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+technology"} quantum technology
                  is in
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+computing"} quantum computing
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+sensors"} quantum sensors
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trapped+ion"} Trapped ions
                  are one of the promising platform for
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+computing"} quantum computing
                  and
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+sensing"} sensing
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+fields"} electromagnetic fields
                  and manipulated using
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lasers"} lasers
                  to perform quantum operations. As a quantum system,
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trapped+ions"} trapped ions
                  offer several advantages. First, they have long coherence times, meaning that the quantum state of the ion can be preserved for a longer period, allowing for more complex calculations. Second,
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trapped+ions"} trapped ions
                  can be precisely controlled and manipulated, allowing for the implementation of high-fidelity quantum gates. Finally,
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trapped+ions"} trapped ions
                  can be entangled with one another, allowing for the implementation of quantum algorithms that are impossible to simulate on classical computers. Trapped ions also have great potential as
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+sensors"} quantum sensors
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/temperature"} temperature
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magnetic+fields"} magnetic fields
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electric+fields"} electric fields
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravitational+wave"} gravitational wave
                  detection, and magnetometry.
                %p
                  One of the major challenges facing trapped ion systems is scalability. While individual ions have been used to perform simple quantum algorithms, scaling the system up to include a large number of ions is a difficult task. However, recent advances in ion trap technology have made it possible to trap larger numbers of ions and transport them in 2D and 3D space to perform more complex operations for quantum computation and sensing experiments. Realization of such devices is not far away. As compared to present atomic clocks, a new generation of quantum-enhanced clocks is now emerging showing significantly improved accuracy. Sensitive physical measurements are an essential component of modern science and technology. Developments in
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+sensors"} quantum sensors
                  will outdate their classical counterparts.
                %p
                  We will present recent developments and opportunities in
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+technology"} quantum technology
                  applications based on
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trapped+ions"} trapped ions
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+computation"} quantum computation
                  and
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+sensing"} sensing
          %p
