              %p
                %strong Detecting topological order from modular transformations of ground states on the torus
              %p
                cf.:
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.04329"} arXiv:2203.04329
              %blockquote
                %p
                  Every two-dimensional
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+phase"} topological phase
                  is associated with some
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+quantum+field+theory"} topological quantum field theory
                  (TQFT), or
                  %a{:href => "modular+tensor+category#AnyonicTopologicalOrderInTermsOfBraidedFusionCategoriesReferences"} more formally
                  a
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modular+tensor+category"} modular tensor category
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ground+states"} ground states
                  of a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+phase"} topological phase
                  %a{:href => "topological+entanglement+entropy+--+references#CharacterizingTopologicalOrder"} encode information
                  about the TQFT, which makes them useful in determining the TQFT data, such as
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anyon"} anyon
                  mutual statistics and self statistics. For example, many numerical methods for detecting the TQFT
                  %a{:href => "topological+entanglement+entropy+--+references#CharacterizingTopologicalOrder"} relied on
                  the use of minimum entanglement states (MESs), which are the eigenstates of the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wilson+loop"} Wilson loop
                  operators, and are labeled by the anyons corresponding to their eigenvalues. Here we revisit the definition of the Wilson loop operators and MESs. We rederive the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modular+transformation"} modular transformation
                  of the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ground+states"} ground states
                  purely from the Wilson loop algebra, and as a result, the modular
                  = succeed "-" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi S
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
                  = succeed "-matrices" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi T
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
          %p
            %br/
