                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/qubits"} qubits
                  or a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/laser"} laser
                  pulse duration.
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/decoherence"} Decoherence
                  is usually a manifestation of the interaction with the environment, and it is an entity of the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+system"} quantum system
                  which generates errors. Small clusters of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/qubits"} qubits
                  with symmetries can be used to shield part of them from
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/decoherence"} decoherence
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gates"} logical gates
                  using 4-level cores with omega-rotation invariance. We show that symmetry renders logical operations particularly resistant to anisotropic qubit rotations that models some quantum errors. We suggest a scalable method for universal quantum processing in which cores act as quansistors, or quantum transistors. By adjusting their intrinsic variables, quansistors may be dynamically isolated from their environment, providing them the adaptability needed to function as controlled
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+memory"} quantum memory
                  units.
