              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Anne+Broadbent"} Anne Broadbent
                (University of Ottowa, Canada)
              %p
                %strong Quantum Delegation with an Off-the-Shelf Device
              %p
                video:
                %a{:href => "https://nyu.zoom.us/rec/share/nzdpfDFPA3928TopuGLljUfHRmbG1UjUOd4Qa1tNqqxDb0btReLKqtfmQQJRF-QR.YZ9wx9uXUevKunh0"} Zm
              %p
                cf.
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.03448"} arXiv:2304.03448
              %blockquote
                %p
                  Given that reliable cloud
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+computers"} quantum computers
                  are becoming closer to reality, the concept of delegation of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+computations"} quantum computations
                  and its
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/software+verification"} verifiability
                  is of central interest. Many models have been proposed, each with specific strengths and weaknesses. Here, we put forth a new model where the client trusts only its classical processing, makes no computational assumptions, and interacts with a quantum server in a single round. In addition, during a set-up phase, the client specifies the size
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                  of the computation and receives an untrusted, off-the-shelf (OTS) quantum device that is used to report the outcome of a single constant-sized measurement from a predetermined logarithmic-sized input. In the OTS model, we thus picture that a single quantum server does the bulk of the computations, while the OTS device is used as an untrusted and generic verification device, all in a single round. In this talk, we will show how the delegation of quantum computations can be achieved in the OTS model, and furthermore how to make this protocol zero-knowledge. The emphasis will be on the concepts that contribute to this result; these concepts are drawn from a long line of research related to blind and delegated quantum computation, as well as quantum zero-knowledge proofs. Based on joint work with Arthur Mehta and Yuming Zhao.
          %p
            %br/
          %ul
            %li#PengFuOct2023
              %p 23 Oct 2023
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frank+%28Peng%29+Fu"} Frank (Peng) Fu
                (Univ. Soth Carolina):
              %p
                %strong Proto-Quipper with Dynamic Lifting
              %p
                video:
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://youtu.be/bBL7rlqbDWM"} YT
                %a{:href => "https://nyu.zoom.us/rec/share/IPDDFaZYw-ZJ-g0NAtJ4fixrd-2hjzZeynyiX5WoTdFe_2jHG88J1pUwF8evjsAI.oe71A2fEQ0QJhqpk"} Zm
              %blockquote
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quipper"} Quipper
                  is a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functional+programming+language"} functional programming language
                  for
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+computing"} quantum computing
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+methods"} formal foundation
                  for Quipper. By virtue of being a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+circuit"} circuit
                  description language, Proto-Quipper has two separate runtimes: circuit generation time and circuit execution time. Values that are known at circuit generation time are called parameters, and values that are known at circuit execution time are called states.
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dynamic+lifting"} Dynamic lifting
                  is an operation that enables a state, such as the result of a
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+measurement"} measurement
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a{:href => "quantum+computation#ClassicalControlQuantumData"} interleave classical and quantum computation
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modalities"} modalities
                  to keep track of the use of dynamic lifting. Then, he will discuss the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
                  for dynamic lifting. Finally, if time permits, Dr. Frank will give some examples of Proto-Quipper-Dyn programs.
