              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Javad+Shabani"} Javad Shabani
                (Center of Quantum Information Physics, NYU):
              %p
                %strong Towards Realization of Protected Qubits Using Topological Superconductivity
              %p
                video:
                %a{:href => "https://nyu.zoom.us/rec/share/qGQmot9lKWVPD6N5J-Ajhx3aqJuig7VaU9qe53q4qM-pFNgb9qCVaY_9u4ivLkA2.eimyjBKMzZlZ37fT?startTime=1701781356000"} Zm
              %p
                cf.:
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "superconductivity#ShabaniEtAl23"} arXiv:2303.04784
                %a{:href => "superconductivity#ShabaniEtAl22"} arXiv:2101.09272
              %blockquote
                %p
                  A central goal in
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+computing"} quantum computing
                  research is to
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+quantum+computation"} protect
                  and
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+error+correction"} control
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+information"} quantum information
                  from
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noise"} noise
                  %a{:href => "superconductivity#AnyonsInTopologicalSuperconductorsReferences"} topological superconductivity
                  where we can encode information in spatially separated
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Majorana+zero+modes"} Majorana zero modes
                  (MZM). We show that
                  %a{:href => "superconductivity#AnyonsInTopologicalSuperconductorsReferences"} topological superconductivity
                  can be achieved in certain hybrid materials where the topological properties are not found in the constituent materials. These special MZMs are formed at the location of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+defects"} topological defects
                  (e.g.
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary+field+theory"} boundaries
                  = succeed ",..)" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/domain+walls"} domain walls
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anyon"} non-Abelian braiding statistics
                  that can be used in noise-free
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gate"} unitary gate
                  operations. We show by engineering a reconfigurable domain wall on a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Josephson+junction"} Josephson junction
                  we can create a scalable platform to study MZM properties and their applications in
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+information"} quantum information
                  science.
          %p
            %br/
          %ul
            %li#RamanathanDec2023
              %p 11 Dec 2023
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chandrasekhar+Ramanathan"} Chandrasekhar Ramanathan
                (Dartmouth College, New Hampshire):
              %p
                %strong Quieting Noisy Neighbors: Extending the Coherence Times of Central Electronic Spins in Solids
              %p
                video:
                %a{:href => "https://youtu.be/FfgT3AAuEt8"} YT
              %p
                cf.:
                %a{:href => "nitrogen-vacancy+center+in+diamond#BSERTRH23"} arXiv:arXiv:2311.05396
              %blockquote
                %p
                  Isolated
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electron"} electronic
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spins"} spins
                  such as donors in silicon and defects like the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nitrogen-vacancy+center+in+diamond"} nitrogen-vacancy (NV) center in diamond
                  are promising platforms for some
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+system"} quantum
                  technologies. The
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/decoherence"} decoherence
                  of these spins is often dominated by interactions with other electronic or nuclear spin species present in their vicinity. For example, silicon-29 nuclear spins can limit the coherence times of donors in silicon, and substitutional nitrogen or P1 centers often limit the coherence times of NV centers in diamond. In this talk I will describe two recent sets of experiments from our group where we are able to extend the coherence times of the central spin by engineering these spin-bath interactions. First, I show how the coherence times of phosphorus donors in silicon are influenced by low-power above-bandgap optical excitation. Next, I describe the use of dynamical decoupling techniques to suppress NV-P1 interactions in diamond. In addition to extending coherence times, these decoupling techniques can be used to measure time-dependent magnetic fields, a form of AC-sensing or noise spectroscopy.
