              %p
                video:
                %a{:href => "https://nyu.zoom.us/rec/share/Y-f2Db6OO5dD-JI3WZhW-CVAWWJqo8IEr_x0cx4f0uvhZu4ahXlJSpWcpjotncN8.jF_oWx6DgV-cSBwb"} Zoom
              %blockquote
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetry+breaking"} Symmetry-breaking
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \;
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+phase+transitions"} quantum phase transitions
                  lead to the production of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+defects"} topological defects
                  or
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/domain+walls"} domain walls
                  in a wide range of
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physical+systems"} physical systems
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bose-Einstein+condensate"} Bose-Einstein condensate
                  as a testbed system where critical scaling behavior in a first-order quantum phase transition can be understood from generic properties. We generalize the Kibble-Zurek mechanism to determine the critical exponents for: (1) the onset of the decay of the metastable state on short times scales, and (2) the number of resulting phase-separated ferromagnetic domains at longer times, as a one-dimensional spin-1 condensate is ramped across a first-order quantum phase transition. The predictions are in excellent agreement with mean-field numerical simulations and provide a paradigm for studying the decay of metastable states in experimentally accessible systems.
          %p
