            %li#HuangOct2024
              %p 21 Oct 2024
              %p Sheng-Jie Huang (Oxford University and Max Planck Institute for the Physics of Complex Systems):
              %p
                %strong Quantum Matter Through the Lens of Topological Holography
              %p
                cf.
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.09611"} arXiv:2405.09611
              %blockquote
                %p
                  It is well known that symmetry offers valuable insights for organizing
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+phases"} quantum phases
                  of matter and leads to important physical consequences, such as [conservation laws]] and constraints on the low-energy dynamics of a
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
                %p
                  Recently, significant progress has been made in generalizing the concept of symmetry and exploring its connection to
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+defects"} topological defects
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+principle"} holographic principle
                  for
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+symmetries"} generalized symmetries
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+order"} topological order
                  in one higher dimension.
                %p
                  This framework decouples the topological data from the local dynamics of the theory and provides a unified description of symmetry and duality in both gapped and gapless phases of matter. Specifically, I will focus on various exotic quantum critical points and gapless phases in (1+1)d, including phase transitions between
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetry+protected+topological+phase"} symmetry-protected topological
                  (SPT) phases, symmetry-enriched quantum critical points, deconfined quantum critical points, and intrinsically gapless SPT phases.
          %p
