              %p
                %a{:href => "https://sites.google.com/view/rishikeshg"} Rishikesh Gajjala
                (NYUAD, CQTS):
              %p
                %strong Designing Quantum Experiments via Graph Theory
              %p
                slides:
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Gajjala-CQTS-May2025.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
              %blockquote
                %p
                  Greenberger–Horne–Zeilinger (GHZ) states are
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+states"} quantum states
                  involving at least three
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+entanglement"} entangled
                  particles. They are foundational in
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+information+theory"} quantum information theory
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+communication"} quantum communication
                  and
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+cryptography"} cryptography
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.06646"} Krenn, Gu, and Zeilinger discovered
                  a correspondence between a class of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+optics"} quantum‐optical
                  experiments that produce GHZ states and edge‐weighted, edge‐coloured
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multigraphs"} multigraphs
                  called
                  = succeed "." do
                    %em GHZ graphs
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertices"} vertices
                  and high dimension would be a major breakthrough, while proving the non‐existence of such graphs (and hence the experiments) has deep implications for quantum resource theory.
                %p
                  In this talk, I will present our results on this
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graph+theory"} graph‐theoretic
                  question and how they translate to quantum experiments. When there is no destructive
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+interference"} interference
                  = succeed "-vertex" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mn 2
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n/2
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mn 1.41
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n/1.41
                  on the dimension for simple GHZ graphs. Krenn and Gu made a stronger conjecture: that the dimension of any GHZ graph with more than four vertices is at most two (i.e., destructive interference does not help). We show that this holds for infinitely many graphs. Namely, graphs of vertex‐connectivity at most two and for all cubic graphs. Moreover, we prove that any minimal counterexample must be 4‑vertex‑connected. These structural insights both deepen our theoretical understanding and guide automated GHZ‐graph searches (e.g., via PyTheus).
          %p
