                  %a{:href => "https://nyu.zoom.us/rec/share/sYGPbeuU0leoUWpzXucAPx9UYVzeb0tN6GjVXalK-5fuKZh8X8czxB-AM6Ky6NDN.c56gKC_K1yxcl-6n"} Zm
                %a{:href => "https://cdnapisec.kaltura.com/p/1674401/sp/167440100/embedIframeJs/uiconf_id/23435151/partner_id/1674401?iframeembed=true&playerId=kaltura_player&entry_id=1_ulzb2kcr"} kt
              %blockquote
                %p
                  In this talk, we will explore a novel approach to study
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry"} supersymmetric
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theories"} quantum field theories
                  using tools from
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+theory"} stable homotopy theory
          %p
            %br/
          %ul
            %li#BunkMar24
              %p 27 Mar 2024
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Severin+Bunk"} Severin Bunk
                (University of Hertfordshire, UK):
              %p
                %strong Infinitesimal Higher Symmetries and Higher Connections
              %p
                slides:
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Bunk-InfinitesimalHigherSymmetries.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
              %blockquote
                %p
                  Every
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bundle"} bundle
                  on a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
                  has a universal
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetry+group"} symmetry group
                  which controls all
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariance"} equivariant
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structures"} structures
                  on the bundle. We modify this idea in two ways: we consider the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal"} infinitesimal
                  version of universal symmetries and allow
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+structure"} higher
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorification"} categorified
                  bundles. These appear, for instance, in
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+gauge+theory"} higher gauge theory
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometry
                  and
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
                  %a{:href => "formal+moduli+problem#RelationToLInfinityAlgebras"} Lurie-Pridham Theorem
                  of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+geometry"} derived
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation+theory"} deformation theory
                  to construct these higher, infinitesimal analogues of universal symmetry groups. We then use this to provide a unified definition of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+smooth+principal+infinity-bundle"} connections on higher bundles
                  and an algebraic formulation of
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential cohomology
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Baez"} Baez
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"} Schreiber
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Konrad+Waldorf"} Waldorf
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mikhail+Kapranov"} Kapranov
                  and others. Here, the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature"} curvature
                  of a higher connection appears as an
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/obstruction"} obstruction
                  to infinitesimal equivariance. We elaborate in particular on the
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+n-bundle+with+connection"}
                      case of higher
                      = succeed "-bundles" do
                        %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                          %semantics
                            %mrow
                              %mi U
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mn 1
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(1)
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bundle+gerbe"} gerbes
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lukas+M%C3%BCller"} Lukas Müller
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Joost+Nuiten"} Joost Nuiten
                  and
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Richard+Szabo"} Richard Szabo
          %p
            %br/
          %h3#apr_2024_3 Apr 2024
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Stottmeister"} Alexander Stottmeister
                (Leibniz Universität Hannover):
              %p
                %strong Embezzlement of entanglement and the classification of von Neumann algebras
              %p
                cf.:
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.07299"} arXiv:2401.07299
              %blockquote
                %p
                  We discuss the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+embezzlement"} embezzlement
                  of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+entanglement"} entanglement
                  in the setting of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/von+Neumann+algebras"} von Neumann algebras
                  and its relation to the classification of the latter. Embezzlement (of entanglement), introduced by van Dam and Hayden, denotes the task of producing any entangled
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+state"} state
                  to arbitrary precision from a shared entangled resource state, the embezzling state, using local operations without communication while perturbing the resource arbitrarily little.
                %p
                  We show that
                  %a{:href => "von+Neumann+algebra+factor#Connes75"} Connes’ classification
                  of
                  %a{:href => "von+Neumann+algebra+factor#TypeIII"} type III von Neumann algebras
                  can be given a quantitative operational interpretation in terms of embezzlement. This quantification implies that all type III factors, apart from some type
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi III
                          %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} III_0
                  factors, host embezzling states. In contrast, semifinite factors (type I or II) cannot host embezzling
          %p
            %br/
          %h3#may_2024_3 May 2024
          %ul
            %li
              %p 01 May 2024
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dmitry+Melnikov"} Dmitry Melnikov
                (Federal University of Rio Grande do Norte, Brazil)
              %p
                %strong Chern-Simons States and Quantum Information
              %p
                video:
                %a{:href => "https://cdnapisec.kaltura.com/html5/html5lib/v2.73.2/mwEmbedFrame.php/p/1674401/uiconf_id/23435151/entry_id/1_6caip54g?wid=_1674401&iframeembed=true&playerId=kaltura_player&entry_id=1_6caip54g"} kt
              %p
                cf.
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.08548"} arXiv:2302.08548
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.16683"} arXiv:2312.16683
              %blockquote
                %p
                  In this talk I will review the properties of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+states"} quantum states
                  in
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theories"} Chern-Simons theories
                  from the perspective of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+quantum+field+theory"} topological quantum field theory
                  paradigm. I will focus on the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SU%282%29"}
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi SU
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 2
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SU(2)
                  theory but the discussion can be generalized to other
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+group"} groups
                  and other
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TQFTs"} TQFTs
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holography"} holography
                  (
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS%2FCFT"}> AdS/CFT
                  ).
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+entanglement"} Quantum entanglement
                  and its properties will be discussed using the topological presentation, and I will give a few examples of the “topological engineering” of quantum resources and protocols. Time permitting I will also mention possible applications of the topological approach to the
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/black+hole+information+paradox"} black hole information paradox
          %p
            %br/
          %ul
            %li#BuijsMay24
              %p 08 May 2024
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urtzi+Buijs"}> Urtzi Buijs
                \:
              %p
                %strong Explicit Quillen models for Cartesian products
              %p
                slides:
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Buijs-QuillenModels2024.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
              %p
                cf.:
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.18168"} arXiv:2402.18168
              %blockquote
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+homotopy+theory"} Rational Homotopy Theory
                  studies the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type"} homotopy type
                  of ‘
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torsion+subgroup"}> torsion
                  \-free’
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sullivan+model"} algebraic model
                  to each space that contains all its rational information. This allows homotopy problems to be addressed computationally.
                %p
                  The use of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+graded+Lie+algebras"} differential graded Lie algebras
                  to model spaces was developed by
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Quillen"} D. Quillen
                  in the 60s of the last centuryowever, despite having algebraic models for each space, in some cases their effective calculation turns out to be very complex.
                %p
                  In this talk we will give explicit minimal Quillen models for the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+topological+space"} Cartesian products
                  of certain spaces in terms of derivations of their models. The model presented also allows us to explicitly describe a model for the
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagonal+map"} diagonal map
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lusternik-Schnirelmann+category"} Lusternik-Schnirelmann category
                  and
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+complexity"} Topological complexity
          %p
            %br/
          %ul
            %li
              %p 13 May 2024
              %p Iman Marivan (Duke University):
              %p
                %em Locality, Symmetry, and Coherence Through the Lens of Quantum Information
              %blockquote
                %p
                  The primary goal of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+information+science"} quantum information science
                  is to harness the power of quantum systems for computing and information-processing tasks. Interestingly, the quantum information perspective also provides new insights and raises new questions about the fundamental concepts of locality, symmetry, and coherence. In the first part of my talk, I will discuss an ongoing project focused on understanding the implications of symmetries and the locality of interactions within the framework of quantum circuits.
          %p
            %br/
          %h3#sep_2024_2 Sep 2024
          %ul
            %li
              %p 04 Sep 2024
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alessandro+Portaluri"} Alessandro Portaluri
                (University of Torino and NYUAD):
              %p
                %strong Index and Stability of (Semi-)Riemannian Closed Geodesics
              %p
                cf.
                %a{:href => "geodesic#PortaluriWuYang21"} arXiv:1907.05864
              %blockquote
                %p
                  A celebrated result proved by
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincar%C3%A9"} Poincaré
                  affirms that a closed non-degenerate minimizing
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geodesic"} geodesic
                  on an
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientation"} oriented
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surface"} surface
                  is hyperbolic. Starting from this classical theorem and by using some recent results on the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maslov+index"} Maslov index
                  and on the Spectral Flow, we will present a general instability criterion for
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/timelike"} timelike
                  and
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacelike"} spacelike
                  closed (semi-)Riemannian geodesics on (non-)oriented
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+manifold"} manifolds
                  of any dimension.
          %p
            %br/
          %ul
            %li#DinarSep24
              %p 09 Sep 2024
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yassir+Dinar"} Yassir Dinar
                (Sultan Qaboos University, Oman, and NYUAD):
              %p
                %strong
                  Dubrovin-Frobenius Manifolds from Classical
                  = succeed "-Algebras" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi W
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
              %p
                slides:
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Dinar-CQTS-Sep-2024.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
              %p
                cf.:
                %a{:href => "Frobenius+manifold#Dinar21"} arXiv:1911.00271
              %blockquote
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dubrovin-Frobenius+manifolds"} Dubrovin-Frobenius manifolds
                  offer a remarkable geometric realization for potentials that satisfy the Witten-Dijkgraaf-Verlinde-Verlinde (WDVV) equations, which describe the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+space"} moduli space
                  of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+TQFT"} two-dimensional
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+field+theories"} topological field theories
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariant+theory"} invariant theory
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+cohomology"} quantum cohomology
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integrable+systems"} integrable systems
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singularity+theory"} singularity theory
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/information+geometry"} information geometry
                %p
                  A key method for constructing
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dubrovin-Frobenius+manifolds"} Dubrovin-Frobenius manifolds
                  lies within the notion of local
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+brackets"} Poisson brackets
                  of hydrodynamic type. Such a bracket can be obtained by considering the leading term of a local Poisson bracket admitting a dispersionless limit. On the other hand, we can construct, beginning from any
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nilpotent+Lie+algebra"} nilpotent
                  element in a
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simple+Lie+algebra"} simple Lie algebra
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
                  known as classical
                  = succeed "-algebra" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi W
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
                %p
                  In this talk, I will present recent findings and address the challenges involved in constructing Dubrovin-Frobenius manifolds from classical
                  = succeed "-algebras." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi W
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
          %p
            %br/
          %ul
            %li#RileySep24
              %p 18 Sep 2024
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mitchell+Riley"} Mitchell Riley
                (CQTS @ NYUAD):
              %p
                %strong Lazy Algorithms in Algebraic Topology
              %p
                cf.
                %a{:href => "https://github.com/mvr/at"} github.com/mvr/at
              %blockquote
                %p
                  Finite
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+complexes"} simplicial complexes
                  are easily represented on a computer as lists of the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplices"} simplices
                  that they contain. In this form, it is not difficult to perform constructions involving them or compute invariants such as their
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+homology"} homology
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+topology"} algebraic topology
                  cannot be represented in this way, and so a new strategy is needed.
                %p
                  %a{:href => "computational+topology#ReferencesComputationalAlgebraicTopology"} Effective Algebraic Topology
                  is a collection of techniques for manipulating certain infinite
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+sets"} simplicial sets
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algorithm"} algorithmically
          %p
            %br/
          %h3#oct_2024_3 Oct 2024
          %ul
            %li#Golub2024
              %p 09 Oct 2024
              %p Nikita Golub (Saint Petersburg State University):
              %p
                %strong Functorial Languages in Homological Algebra
              %p
                slides:
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Golub-CQTSOct2024.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
              %p
                cf.:
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.05708"} arXiv:2410.05708
              %blockquote
                %p
                  In our talk, we shall dive into a number of phenomena related to the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+limit"} higher limit
                  approach to the description of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functors"} functors
                  of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+algebra"} homological
                  nature. The first examples appeared in the work of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Quillen"} Quillen
                  on
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hochschild+homology"} Hochschild homology
                  of algebras, where he showed that Hochschild homology of an algebra can be represented as limits of some functors from the category of free
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+extension"} extensions
                  of a given algebra. Further similar formulas
                  %a{:href => "fr-code#IvanovMikhailov15"} were derived
                  by
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Roman+Mikhailov"} Roman Mikhailov
                  and
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sergey+Ivanov"} Sergey Ivanov
                  in the context of
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+homology"} group homology
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fr-language"} fr-language
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi f
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{f}
                  and
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi r
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{r}
                  are some functorial
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ideals"} ideals
                  of the functor of
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+rings"} group rings
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi f
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi r
                          %mo +
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi r
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi f
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{f}\mathbf{r} + \mathbf{r}\mathbf{f}
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi r
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi r
                          %mo +
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi f
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi r
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi f
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{r}\mathbf{r} + \mathbf{f}\mathbf{r}\mathbf{f}
                %p
                  In our work, we have provided a formalisation of their construction and constructed new languages using the
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lower+central+series"} lower central series
                %p
                  We will lift all constructions to spectra and demonstrate that functorial languages on a given manifold of groups (or simply subcategories of a category of groups) give rise to some
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+infinity-categories"} stable infinity-categories
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+K-theory"} algebraic K-theory
                  to functorial surfaces, we arrive at flux spectra. This may provide a bridge between
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+theory"} group theory
                  and
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+theory"} stable homotopy theory
          %p
            %br/
          %ul
            %li
              %p 23 Oct 2024
              %p Nizar Demni (Aix-Marseille Université (AMU) France and NYUAD):
              %p
                %strong From the Dunkl Intertwining Operator to Simple Hurwitz Numbers
              %p
                slides:
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Demni-Dunkl-Oct2024.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
              %blockquote
                %p
                  I’ll start with a review of finite
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/root+systems"} root systems
                  and their related matters (Weyl chambers, reflection groups, multiplicity functions). Then, I’ll introduce the Dunkl derivatives and the Dunkl intertwining operator. In this context, I’ll discuss the commutativity of the former and the existence of the latter, relying on a deformed
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+cohomology"} de Rham cohomology
                  and the reflection
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+algebra"} group algebra
          %p
            %br/
          %h3#nov_2024_3 Nov 2024
          %ul
            %li#BeniniNov2024
              %p 06 Nov 2024
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marco+Benini"} Marco Benini
                (University of Genova, Italy):
              %p
                %strong A stacky approach to the comparison of axiomatizations of quantum field theory
              %p
                slides:
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Benini-CQTS-Nov2024.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
              %blockquote
                %p
                  After a gentle introduction to
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relativistic+field+theory"} Lorentzian
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
                  as axiomatized by
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+quantum+field+theory"} algebraic quantum field theory
                  (Haag-Kastler, Brunetti-Fredenhagen-Verch, …) and by
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/factorization+algebras"} factorization algebras
                  (Costello-Gwilliam, …), we shall present a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} categorical equivalence
                  between these two axiomatic approaches that relies crucially on the intricate interplay between the principles of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causality"} causality
